1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,272
A city is split into blocks.

2
00:00:02,272 --> 00:00:04,276
A school is split into sections.

3
00:00:04,276 --> 00:00:07,876
A market is split into aisles,
each for one kind of thing.

4
00:00:08,276 --> 00:00:12,608
Split a big crowd by kind, and
it becomes easy to find your way.

5
00:00:12,608 --> 00:00:15,036
The periodic table does exactly this.

6
00:00:15,486 --> 00:00:17,738
Diya sees it on her kitchen shelf.

7
00:00:17,738 --> 00:00:21,889
Spices on one rack, grains on
another, steel vessels on a third.

8
00:00:22,239 --> 00:00:25,006
The periodic table has
racks like these too.

9
00:00:25,006 --> 00:00:26,941
Four of them, called blocks.

10
00:00:27,441 --> 00:00:31,753
By the end of this video, you will
tell an element's block from one clue:

11
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:33,568
where its last electron goes.

12
00:00:34,218 --> 00:00:35,741
First, a quick map.

13
00:00:35,741 --> 00:00:37,174
A period is a row.

14
00:00:37,174 --> 00:00:40,536
All elements in a period
fill the same outer shell.

15
00:00:40,886 --> 00:00:42,445
A group is a column.

16
00:00:42,445 --> 00:00:46,631
All elements in a group have the
same outer electron arrangement.

17
00:00:46,981 --> 00:00:49,745
So sodium and potassium share group one.

18
00:00:49,745 --> 00:00:54,103
Each has one outer electron, and
both react strongly with water.

19
00:00:54,503 --> 00:00:55,717
Now the blocks.

20
00:00:55,717 --> 00:01:00,161
A block is named after the orbital
that receives the last electron.

21
00:01:00,161 --> 00:01:01,756
s, p, d, or f.

22
00:01:02,406 --> 00:01:04,687
The block widths are not random.

23
00:01:04,687 --> 00:01:09,695
An s subshell holds two electrons, p
holds six, d holds ten, and f holds

24
00:01:09,695 --> 00:01:10,595
fourteen.

25
00:01:10,479 --> 00:01:14,774
So the blocks are two, six,
ten, and fourteen columns wide.

26
00:01:15,424 --> 00:01:18,863
Let's watch the last electron
on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:18,863 --> 00:01:21,653
Start with sodium, atomic number eleven.

28
00:01:22,003 --> 00:01:24,478
Ten electrons fill the inner shells.

29
00:01:24,478 --> 00:01:26,748
The eleventh drops into three s.

30
00:01:26,748 --> 00:01:28,875
So sodium is in the s block.

31
00:01:29,275 --> 00:01:31,809
The s block is groups one and two.

32
00:01:31,809 --> 00:01:34,562
Outer arrangement n s one or n s two.

33
00:01:34,562 --> 00:01:36,428
These are reactive metals.

34
00:01:37,078 --> 00:01:38,884
Now chlorine, seventeen.

35
00:01:38,884 --> 00:01:41,514
Its last electron drops into three p.

36
00:01:41,514 --> 00:01:43,883
That makes it a p block element.

37
00:01:44,283 --> 00:01:47,124
The p block is groups
thirteen to eighteen.

38
00:01:47,124 --> 00:01:49,126
n s two, n p one to six.

39
00:01:49,126 --> 00:01:54,137
It holds metals, non metals, metalloids,
and at the end, the noble gases.

40
00:01:54,787 --> 00:01:56,640
Now iron, twenty six.

41
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:00,890
After four s fills, its last
electrons go into three d.

42
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,049
Iron is a d block element.

43
00:02:03,449 --> 00:02:07,488
The d block is groups three to
twelve, the transition elements.

44
00:02:07,488 --> 00:02:12,187
For them, the group number is the d
electrons plus the outer s electrons.

45
00:02:12,587 --> 00:02:14,073
Last, the f block.

46
00:02:14,073 --> 00:02:16,636
Its last electron enters an f orbital.

47
00:02:16,636 --> 00:02:21,283
These are the lanthanoids and the
actinoids, the inner transition elements,

48
00:02:21,283 --> 00:02:23,447
in the two rows at the bottom.

49
00:02:24,097 --> 00:02:25,543
Now one odd case.

50
00:02:25,543 --> 00:02:27,215
Helium is one s two.

51
00:02:27,215 --> 00:02:31,422
Its last electron enters an s
orbital, just like beryllium.

52
00:02:31,822 --> 00:02:32,983
Pause and think.

53
00:02:32,983 --> 00:02:37,024
So why does helium sit in group
eighteen, and not in group two?

54
00:02:40,224 --> 00:02:41,847
Its only shell is full!

55
00:02:41,847 --> 00:02:46,371
Like the other noble gases, helium
has a closed shell, so it behaves like

56
00:02:46,371 --> 00:02:47,271
them.

57
00:02:47,556 --> 00:02:48,726
Remember this.

58
00:02:48,726 --> 00:02:52,553
The block is decided by
where the last electron goes.

59
00:02:53,253 --> 00:02:55,108
Now, one worked example.

60
00:02:55,108 --> 00:02:58,021
Place four elements by
their configurations.

61
00:02:58,321 --> 00:02:59,221
First.

62
00:02:58,987 --> 00:03:01,289
Neon core, then three s one.

63
00:03:01,289 --> 00:03:02,860
Last electron in s.

64
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:06,232
So it is s block, period three, group one.

65
00:03:06,232 --> 00:03:07,132
Sodium.

66
00:03:07,702 --> 00:03:08,602
Second.

67
00:03:08,428 --> 00:03:11,486
Neon core, three s two, three p four.

68
00:03:11,486 --> 00:03:13,070
Last electron in p.

69
00:03:13,070 --> 00:03:14,929
p block, period three.

70
00:03:14,929 --> 00:03:17,800
Six outer electrons, so group sixteen.

71
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:18,700
Sulphur.

72
00:03:19,331 --> 00:03:20,231
Third.

73
00:03:19,988 --> 00:03:22,984
Argon core, three d five, four s two.

74
00:03:22,984 --> 00:03:24,536
Last electron in d.

75
00:03:24,536 --> 00:03:26,303
d block, period four.

76
00:03:26,303 --> 00:03:29,299
Five plus two is seven, so group seven.

77
00:03:29,299 --> 00:03:30,199
Manganese.

78
00:03:30,922 --> 00:03:31,822
Fourth.

79
00:03:31,669 --> 00:03:34,761
Xenon core, four f seven, six s two.

80
00:03:34,761 --> 00:03:36,392
Last electron in f.

81
00:03:36,392 --> 00:03:38,306
f block, a lanthanoid.

82
00:03:38,306 --> 00:03:39,206
Europium.

83
00:03:39,916 --> 00:03:41,382
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:41,732 --> 00:03:46,282
Many students see iron as four s
two, and call it an s block element.

85
00:03:46,682 --> 00:03:51,204
But the block follows the last
electron, not the outermost shell.

86
00:03:51,204 --> 00:03:53,708
Iron's last electrons enter three d.

87
00:03:53,708 --> 00:03:55,300
So iron is d block.

88
00:03:55,951 --> 00:03:57,463
Where is this asked?

89
00:03:57,463 --> 00:04:02,059
As short questions on locating an
element by its block, period and group

90
00:04:02,059 --> 00:04:03,663
from its configuration.

91
00:04:04,263 --> 00:04:05,472
Exam level now.

92
00:04:05,472 --> 00:04:09,846
An element has the outer configuration
three d five, four s one.

93
00:04:09,846 --> 00:04:12,120
Which block and group is it in?

94
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:13,380
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:19,916
The answer is B.

96
00:04:19,916 --> 00:04:21,273
d block, group six.

97
00:04:21,773 --> 00:04:24,124
The last electron enters three d.

98
00:04:24,124 --> 00:04:26,226
So it is a d block element.

99
00:04:26,726 --> 00:04:32,050
For the d block, group number equals the
d electrons plus the outer s electrons.

100
00:04:32,550 --> 00:04:34,264
Five plus one is six.

101
00:04:34,264 --> 00:04:36,127
Group six, period four.

102
00:04:36,127 --> 00:04:38,333
D forgot the four s electron.

103
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:40,389
Here is your recap.

104
00:04:40,389 --> 00:04:41,886
Screenshot this card.

105
00:04:45,086 --> 00:04:48,084
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

106
00:04:48,484 --> 00:04:49,384
One.

107
00:04:49,052 --> 00:04:52,383
Why is the p block
exactly six columns wide?

108
00:04:53,183 --> 00:04:54,083
Two.

109
00:04:53,712 --> 00:04:58,587
Why is iron a d block element, even
though its outer shell is four s?

110
00:04:59,387 --> 00:05:00,287
Three.

111
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:03,318
In which block are the
lanthanoids and actinoids?

112
00:05:04,218 --> 00:05:09,300
Next time: can you find an element's
exact address from its electrons alone?

113
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:13,569
We find out in electronic
configuration and position of elements.
